  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nA low pressure system is slowly traveling through the central \npart of the United States. Showers and thunderstorms will push \nthrough central and southeastern IL again today, but won't be a \ncomplete washout as there will be breaks in the rainfall. The \nnext round of showers and storms will develop later this morning\nas the low pivots into the area. Coverage will be scattered. \nOutside of any storms, skies will be cloudy. The greatest \nrainfall amounts won't be widespread and will be fairly \nlocalized. As of this morning, MRMS is estimating some localized\npockets of 1-2 inches across central and southeastern IL in the\nlast 24 hours. In contrast, some locations have seen less than \na quarter inch in that same time period. WPC has us in a slight \nrisk ERO for today for areas east of I-55 and north of I-70. \nFlash flooding could occur where grounds are already saturated \nor after multiple rounds of heavy rain over the same locations. \n\nAs the low pressure center moves directly overhead throughout the \nday, the non-supercell tornado parameter will increase as localized \nmaximums of low-level SRH pivot over the central part of the state. \nSomeone may see a funnel cloud (or multiple) across central IL. \nOutside of thunderstorms, winds will be quite gusty from the \ntightening pressure gradient. The HREF shows a 60-80% chance of wind \ngusts greater than 35 mph west of I-55 during the afternoon and \nevening hours. \n\nTemperatures will be unseasonably cool with highs in the 70s today. \nTemperatures gradually increase solidly back into the 80s by midweek \nnext week. Lows will be in the 60s for the next week before warming \ninto the 70s by Friday night. Weather will dry out for Monday and \nTuesday, but additional rain chances muddle the new week starting \nWednesday. There are low (~20%) chance for showers and thunderstorms \noff and on throughout next week from the timing uncertainty of when \nan upper level shortwave will push into Illinois, dragging a cold \nfront along with it.",
